X-SOURCE:Meetup: Raleigh AI, ML & Computer Vision
UID:event_317517575@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261128T073103Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270121T140000
SUMMARY:Jan 21 - AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics acro
 ss AI\, ML\, and computer vision.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nJan 2
 1\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https
 ://voxel51.com/events/ai-ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-january-21-2027)**\
 n\n**Verifier-Gated Generative Tutoring: When the LLM Writes Lessons but N
 ever Decides Mastery**\n\nAdaptive tutors increasingly let LLMs both gener
 ate content and decide mastery — a risky coupling. dino.study separates 
 those roles: models draft interactive lessons under verifier gates\, while
  deterministic Python BKT + FSRS tracks mastery and review timing. I'll sh
 ow the production architecture (Expo app + Bun API + Postgres prerequisite
  graphs)\, cost (\\~$0.04/lesson)\, and an honest LLM-simulated N=30/arm d
 ry-run that is not a human peer-reviewed study.\n\nThe talk is code-forwar
 d with a live demo. Aimed at ML/CV practitioners curious about evaluation\
 , mastery modeling\, and shipping educational AI.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n
 \n[Animesh Alang](https://www.linkedin.com/in/animesh-alang-232713132) is 
 a UCSC Computer Engineering junior building dino.study. He founded SlugAI\
 , serves as President of CruzHacks\, and was Cloud Track winner at NVIDIA 
 Hack-a-Claw.\n\n**Building CAD for Machine Intelligence: The Future of AI-
 Driven Engineering**\n\nWhat does it take to build AI systems that can rea
 son about geometry\, design mechanical parts\, and automate engineering wo
 rkflows? In this talk\, we introduce Quartz\, a CAD system built for machi
 ne intelligence\, and explore how high-performance geometry execution\, re
 inforcement learning\, and massively parallel environments enable AI agent
 s to learn engineering through design and iteration.\n\nWe demonstrate how
  AI can create and modify complex assemblies while preserving editable CAD
  histories\, and share our vision for the future of AI-driven engineering.
 \n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Debao Huang](https://www.linkedin.com/in/debao
 -huang/) is currently a Research Scientist at Spectral Labs\, where he dev
 elops AI-native CAD systems and foundation models for automating engineeri
 ng design. He obtained his Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering fr
 om The Ohio State University.\n\n**Does 70% Mean 70%? Building an Honest T
 ennis Win-Probability Engine**\n\nWhen a model says a tennis player has a 
 70% chance to win\, does that player win 70% of the time? I built Tiebreak
 \, a win-probability engine trained on about 900\,000 ATP matches that lay
 ers surface-adjusted Elo\, a point-by-point Markov chain and gradient boos
 ting\, updating live as a match unfolds.\n\nEarly versions looked better t
 han they were because the same match leaked into both training and test da
 ta\, and I show exactly how I caught it with a chronological\, match-level
  split. Reliability diagrams then show where the model is calibrated and w
 here it is overconfident.\n\nAttendees leave with a practical checklist fo
 r building and honestly evaluating any ML model that outputs probabilities
 .\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Krrish Ghindani](https://www.linkedin.com/in/
 krrishghindani/) is a Data Science junior at the University of San Francis
 co on a full merit scholarship and leads USF's Data Science team at nation
 al competitions (1st place).\n\n**Can an Open-Weight Model Power a Physica
 l AI Agent? Serving Gemma 4 on Google TPUs with Voxel51**\n\nIn this sessi
 on\, we'll deploy Google's Gemma 4 on Cloud TPUs using vLLM-TorchTPU and c
 onnect it to the Voxel51 Agent. Through a live demo and inference benchmar
 ks\, we'll explore how open-weight models can support Physical AI workflow
 s\, examining latency\, throughput\, and the infrastructure tradeoffs behi
 nd building reliable AI agents.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Vera](ht
 tps://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) is a Machine Learning Engineer and
  DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of experience building computer visio
 n and machine learning models using TensorFlow\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
