X-SOURCE:Meetup: Raleigh AI, ML & Computer Vision
UID:event_317517754@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261128T073103Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270127T140000
SUMMARY:Jan 27 - Physical AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup: Purpose-Buil
 t Robots
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  us for the Physical AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup series focused on 
 robotics use cases. In this session we will focus on purpose-built robots.
 \n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nJan 27\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\
 nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/physical-ai
 -ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-purpose-built-robots-january-27-2027)**\n\n
 **Building a Home Health Robot for Family Caregiving**\n\nHome health is o
 ne of the most personal places a robot can work\, and one of the least exp
 lored. Jacob will share how Rhem Labs is building a purpose-built tabletop
  robot and family app that help households keep track of health readings a
 nd daily routines\, so changes in a loved one's health can be spotted earl
 ier.\n\nHe will talk about what his own family's caregiving experience tau
 ght him about the problem\, the design choices that come with building for
  older adults and caregivers\, and how a robot earns trust in someone's ho
 me. Attendees will hear how a focused\, single-purpose robot can make a re
 al difference in everyday care.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Jacob Shen](htt
 ps://www.linkedin.com/in/jacobshenn/) is founder and CEO of Rhem Labs\, a 
 Y Combinator company building Rhem\, a tabletop robot for home health. Aft
 er years of caring for relatives at home\, he set out to build a robot and
  family app that track health readings and daily routines so families can 
 spot changes earlier.\n\n**Closing the Loop Between Sensing and Action in 
 Surgical Robotics**\n\nSome of the hardest robotics problems live inside t
 he human body\, where space is tight\, visibility is limited and every mil
 limeter counts. Ali will share how Mosaic Intelligence Labs is building pu
 rpose-built surgical robots that pair sensing catheters with robotic contr
 ol to reduce reliance on X-ray guidance. She will explain why today's inst
 ruments lack a real feedback loop between what they sense and how they act
 \, and how closing that loop opens the door to safer\, more precise proced
 ures. The talk will also cover what it takes to design a robot for a singl
 e\, high-stakes job and the lessons that carry over to purpose-built robot
 s in other fields.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Ali Mackanic](https://www.li
 nkedin.com/in/a-kight/) is co-founder and Chief Technology Officer of Mosa
 ic Intelligence Labs\, a San Francisco company building sensing surgical r
 obots. Her team is developing sensing catheters and robotic controls desig
 ned to reduce reliance on X-ray guidance during procedures\, giving surgic
 al instruments a real feedback loop between what they sense and how they a
 ct.\n\n**Purpose-Built for People: The Invisible Metrics of Robot Adoption
 **\n\nYour robot works. Accuracy is 0.1 mm\, uptime is 24/7\, the demo was
  flawless. Six months later\, it sits switched off in a corner of the plan
 t.\n\nRobotics projects rarely fail on engineering metrics. They fail on t
 he ones nobody measures: how much operators trust the robot\, how a single
  bug is perceived\, whether the robot solves the real bottleneck. Purpose-
 built robots have an edge here: designed around operators' real pain point
 s\, they start with trust instead of fighting for it.\n\nDrawing on deploy
 ments in mining\, oil and gas\, manufacturing and pharma\, Hugo Turlet wal
 ks through a real ROI breakdown\, from OEE to adoption\, and shows which h
 idden factors purpose-built design can move. Attendees will leave with a s
 hort list of invisible metrics\, how to measure them\, and how purpose-bui
 lt design can move them.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Hugo Turlet](https://w
 ww.linkedin.com/in/hugo-turlet/) is a robotics engineer who leads the robo
 tics practice at Osedea\, a Montreal innovation firm. His strength is tran
 slation: he turns field needs into technical requirements\, and those requ
 irements into business models that work. A regular robotics and AI columni
 st on Montreal radio and an open-source contributor to Hugging Face\, he w
 orks to make robotics accessible to everyone.\n\n**Motivation and Challeng
 es in working with Multimodal Timeseries Data**\n\nPhysical AI is having i
 ts moment\, with many companies and research teams focussed on this space.
  But working with physical AI data means wrestling with high cardinality\,
  complex\, non-synced\, multi sensor streams that are hard to explore\, al
 ign and curate. In this talk\, we will break down the challenges that come
  with multimodal time series data\, and then look at the research directio
 ns this industry is pursuing\, the ones that are unlocked when you can act
 ually work with your data effectively.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Prerna D
 hareshwar](https://www.linkedin.com/in/prernamd/) is a Machine Learning En
 gineer at Voxel51\, where she helps customers leverage Voxel51 to accelera
 te dataset curation\, model development\, and evaluation in real-world AI 
 workflows.\n\n**Faster Physical AI Development with CoreWeave**\n\nBuildin
 g robots and autonomous systems means training on huge multimodal datasets
 \, running simulation at scale\, and iterating on models quickly. In this 
 short session\, CoreWeave's Head of Physical AI shares how teams are build
 ing their physical AI infrastructure today\, where the bottlenecks are\, a
 nd what is coming next to make it easier to go from data to deployed model
 s.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Matt Shaffer](https://www.linkedin.com/in/sh
 affermatthew/) is a founder and research scientist\, I have spent the last
  decade bridging the gap between frontier research and commercial reality.
  With a background spanning $44M+ in fundraising\, product strategy\, cust
 omer discovery\, research\, engineering\, hiring\, executive operations\, 
 and technical leadership\, I have built and led distributed organizations 
 to deliver AI-powered robotics across industries.
