BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: Raleigh AI, ML & Computer Vision
UID:event_317518807@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261128T073103Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270210T140000
SUMMARY:Feb 10 - Physical AI Meetup: Robot Brains and Models
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  us for the Physical AI\, ML and Computer Vision Meetup series focused on 
 robotics use cases. In this session we will focus on robot brains and mode
 ls.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nFeb 10\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM P
 ST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/physical
 -ai-ml-and-computer-vision-meetup-robot-brains-and-models-february-10-2027
 )**\n\n**Physical AI That Adapts**\n\nEvery vision model is trained on a s
 napshot of the world\, but robots are deployed into one that keeps changin
 g: lighting shifts\, friction drifts\, objects move\, actuators wear. Toda
 y the fix is a slow loop of collecting new data\, retraining\, validating 
 and redeploying\, which can take weeks or months. In this talk\, we show h
 ow Lazy Dynamics’ LazyInfer closes that gap by treating perception\, par
 ameter learning and control as one Bayesian inference problem that runs on
  the device\, inside the control loop.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Bart van
  Erp](https://www.linkedin.com/in/bart-van-erp/) is CTO at Lazy Dynamics. 
 He has a background in Electrical Engineering and a PhD in the field of pr
 obabilistic machine learning.\n\n**Robotic World Models: Cloud Versus On-E
 dge**\n\nCloud-hosted robotic world models can draw on substantial compute
 \, while on-edge models promise local\, low-latency inference\; choosing b
 etween them depends on the reliability of their predictions. We examine th
 is question through a from-scratch\, gradient-verified reproduction of the
  proprioceptive Robotic World Model for ANYmal D\, using recorded robot tr
 ajectories and CPU resources. Autoregressive training reproduces the model
 's long-horizon advantage over teacher forcing\, while ensemble disagreeme
 nt tracks the ordering of prediction errors but substantially underestimat
 es their magnitude. On the released checkpoint's training episodes\, disag
 reement is 33.4 times smaller than realized error at the method's 100-step
  rollout horizon\, making it a useful ranking signal but not a calibrated 
 error bar.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\nJoyjeet Singh is an independent resea
 rcher in physics-informed machine learning and the sole author of three ar
 Xiv preprints on implicit-reasoning models\, world-model reproducibility\,
  and the planning objectives used over learned latent dynamics.\n\n**Faste
 r Physical AI Development with CoreWeave**\n\nRobot brains are getting big
 ger. Vision language action models\, world models and foundation policies 
 all demand large training runs\, fast experimentation and serving that kee
 ps up with real robots. In this session\, Narendra Kumar Vadapalli\, Senio
 r Specialist Field Engineer at CoreWeave\, walks through how physical AI t
 eams are building the infrastructure behind these models today. Drawing on
  his work helping customers design and scale AI platforms on Kubernetes\, 
 he will cover how teams organize multimodal training data\, run distribute
 d training and simulation side by side\, and move models from experiment t
 o deployment without rebuilding their stack each time. Attendees will leav
 e with practical patterns for scaling robot learning workloads and a look 
 at what is coming next.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Narendra Kumar Vadapall
 i](https://www.linkedin.com/in/narenandu/) is a Senior Specialist Field En
 gineer at CoreWeave\, where he has helped customers design and scale AI in
 frastructure since 2024.\n\n**Robot Brains and Models with Voxel51**\n\n*A
 bout the Speaker*\n\n[Brent Griffin](https://www.linkedin.com/in/brent-gri
 ffin-0b6325b5/) is a Principal Machine Learning Scientist at Voxel51\, spe
 cializing in low-cost learning on unstructured data at scale. He previousl
 y built and led the perception group at Agility Robotics and was an assist
 ant research scientist at the University of Michigan\, working at the inte
 rsection of computer vision\, control\, and robot learning.
