SUMMARY:Feb 25 - MCP\, Agents and Skills Meetup
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics acro
 ss MCP\, agents and skills.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nFeb 25\, 20
 27\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https://vox
 el51.com/events/mcp-agents-skills-meetup-february-25-2027)**\n\n**Your Age
 nt Crashed After a Tool Call. Now What?**\n\nAgent demos rarely show what 
 happens when the worker dies halfway through a tool workflow. I’ll demon
 strate crash recovery with LangGraph and Pydantic AI using Azure Durable T
 ask Scheduler. I’ll show persisted progress\, retries\, and a human appr
 oval step. I’ll distinguish workflow recovery from external effects: pre
 venting duplicate effects requires idempotency enforced by the external AP
 I.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Tamir Dresher](https://www.linkedin.com/in/t
 amirdresher/) is a Principal Engineer on Microsoft's Azure Serverless team
 . Tamir built the official Azure Durable Task Scheduler integrations for L
 angGraph and Pydantic AI.\n\n**Before You Connect That MCP Server: What It
  Can Reach\, What It Can Do**\n\nConnect an agent to an MCP server and tha
 t server gets access to your credentials and a standing invitation to act 
 for you. Independent researcher Syed Anas Mohiuddin is credited on 16 GitH
 ub security advisories for MCP servers\, covering server-side request forg
 ery\, command injection\, missing authentication\, session hijack\, and cr
 edential leaks.\n\nThis talk goes through the checks to run before you con
 nect a server: what it can reach on the network\, what it stores and logs\
 , and which of its tools can change real systems. It then covers the check
  that has to sit at run time\, between the agent and the action\, so a poi
 soned tool description or a forged request meets a person before it reache
 s production.\n\nAttendees get a short list of questions to put to any MCP
  server they already run.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Michael Kantor](https
 ://www.linkedin.com/in/kantorcodes) is President of HOL\, a nonprofit cons
 ortium that builds open standards for AI agent infrastructure. HOL's produ
 ct is HOL Guard\, a firewall for any AI agent\, which checks risky actions
  before they run. On 10 November 2026 he spoke online at [re]aligned\, a C
 anSecWest extension\, on testing runtime controls for agents: "Did the Age
 nt Guard Stop the Action\, or Only the Words?"\n\n**Flash Demo: Agents in 
 Action with Voxel51**\n\nIn this flash demo\, Adonai will show some of the
  agent workflows the Voxel51 team has been building\, and how you can put 
 agents to work on your own visual AI data with Voxel51. Expect a quick\, h
 ands-on look at what's possible today and how to try it yourself.\n\n*Abou
 t the Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Vera](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) 
 is a Machine Learning Engineer and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of 
 experience building computer vision and machine learning models using Tens
 orFlow\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
